## Conclusion

Typing `https://www.google.com` into a browser and pressing Enter triggers a complex sequence of operations.

The browser first uses DNS to find the IP address associated with the domain name. It then establishes a TCP/IP connection with the destination and uses HTTPS to securely communicate with the server.

Firewalls help protect the infrastructure, while load balancers distribute traffic across backend servers. Web servers receive HTTP requests and serve static content or forward dynamic requests to application servers. Application servers execute business logic and communicate with databases when persistent data is required.

Finally, the response travels back to the browser, where the browser processes and renders the web page.

Understanding this complete workflow is important because it connects many fundamental concepts of web development, networking, system administration, security, and software engineering.
